  "original_source_text": "Conventional SRAMs are designed for random access, resulting in significant energy waste under regular access patterns common in image processing, computer vision, deep learning, and dense linear algebra to name a few. We propose an energy-efficient ARC-SRAM subarray architecture that integrates three synergetic energy reduction schemes: (1) Address decoding energy reduction by relocating the address generation to the memory periphery; (2) Read energy reduction by reducing wordline activity to one pulse per row and minimizing precharge activity to suppress half-selection energy; (3) Write energy reduction by reusing the bitline charge across consecutive rows, and by minimizing wordline activation. We implement a 64KB instance of our ARC-SRAM using an advanced gate all around nanosheet 1.4 nm research process design kit. At the cell-array level, the design achieves a 63–76\\% energy reduction for reads and 68–72\\% for writes in loop-nest dominated applications, with only 4\\% area overhead.",
